    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is wired into the right units \u2014 the format logic stays in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`, and `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber` correctly needs only a doc update since it builds off the already-assigned `customerCode` string. The OpenAI spec/examples are kept in sync. One structural issue:\n\n- **Duplicated \"first three letters, upper-cased\" idiom** in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.service`:\n  ```java\n  String city3 = city.substring(0, Math.min(3, city.length())).toUpperCase();\n  ...\n  String last3 = lastName.substring(0, Math.min(3, lastName.length())).toUpperCase();\n  ```\n  These two lines are the same transformation applied to two fields. The spec itself defines CITY3 and LAST3 with identical derivation rules, so the shared concept (\"upper-cased first three letters\") deserves a single named unit. Extract a small private helper, e.g.:\n  ```java\n  private static String first3Upper(String s) {\n      return s.substring(0, Math.min(3, s.length())).toUpperCase();\n  }\n  ```\n  and call `first3Upper(city)` / `first3Upper(lastName)`. This removes the duplication, names the intent, and keeps the two segments guaranteed-consistent if the rule ever changes (length, locale on `toUpperCase`, etc.).\n\nNothing else about altitude or placement is off \u2014 the per-city sequence filter correctly lives alongside the code assembly, and no class is turning into a catch-all.",
